Subject: [PATCH 147/217] survey(q8): harden AOCL build evidence and provider
 probes (vendor-gemm-survey)

Follow-ups after the first build window and the first probe runs:

* scripts/vendor/aocl_build_report.py - standalone, light evidence generator
  (repo/tag/commit, configure/make lines, toolchain, generated-header proof of
  the explicit Zen4 config + active dim_t/BLAS-int width, per-file sha256,
  aocl_gemm_* exports, zen4 symbols, vpddbusd disassembly count, kernel
  objects).  Replaces the shell-heredoc report step, which was observed to be
  killed (SIGPIPE/141) leaving the build complete but the report missing.
* scripts/vendor/q8_full_heavy.sh - one-window chain: light report, probes,
  then the matrix; the report step is non-fatal.
* scripts/vendor/run_heavy_with_retry.sh - also retry the signal exit codes
  (137/141/143) the lock waiter has been observed to surface.
* scripts/perf/aocl_semantics_probe.py - fixed post-op construction (SCALE
  needs zero_point_len 1 or >= n; group scales are aocl_pre_op_sf structs),
  the numpy 2.x int() on a (1,1) array, E2 now really exceeds 2**24, and added
  E10_bitwise_envelope (the machine-checkable exactness envelope that backs the
  contract rule) + E7b multi-group layout proof.
* scripts/perf/w8a8_vendor_parity_probe.py - fail closed when the sym_quant
  reorder API returns buffer size 0 (K % 4 != 0), record the f32-block model
  comparison and the int32-wrap picture for the overflow case.
* scripts/perf/vendor_gemm_matrix.py - module-level numpy, prepared zero-arg
  callables (no per-call struct building), artifact/threads/precision metadata,
  incremental result dumps, fail-closed sym_quant reorder check, dim_t
  resolution from the classic-path generated header.
* scripts/perf/summarize_matrix.py - adds the exact(1T) vs bounded(6T) int8
  trade-off table requested by the parent agent.
* docs/pypto_x/0001-...-gemm-provider-contract.zh-CN.md - path A/B table with
  the measured tolerance classes (int32_f32block_bounded), the K % 4 and
  per-row-SCALE fail-closed rules, and the tightened bitwise trigger.
